well we've been really focusing on this  uh ukrainian offensive in the northeast  because uh initially it appears that it  is one of the biggest uh military  counter offenses in europe uh since  world war ii and it's been remarkable at  how fast the ukrainians have been able  to retake land and towns in the  northeast with this offensive that we  will continue to watch closely but while  we are watching that further to the  south at europe's biggest nuclear plant  the zapariza nuclear plant that is in  ukrainian territory but has been  controlled by russian forces for months  now  the ukrainian uh ukrainian nuclear  agency is informing us now that they  have shut down that power plant shut  down the power supply there let me  explain what they essentially have done  is in the middle of the night the the  sixth reactor lost external power due to  shelling early on sunday morning the  ukrainian operators decided to  intentionally for safety reasons shut  down that sixth uh  nuclear generator after they were able  to restore external power it's a process  called islanding  and what they are doing in the short  term it's to to decrease the risk of an  unintentional nuclear accident but in  the long term it is still very  precarious because it is not a process  uh that is normally used in a in a  normal situation it is normally used  only in emergencies so moving forward  should the the nuclear power plant in  zapposia need more power  besides the internal power that they  have now to shut down the systems they  would have to do that with diesel  generators that is clearly not an ideal  situation but they felt they needed to  essentially shut down the nuclear power  plant for safety reasons so as we focus  on the fighting in the northeast and the  south still uh the zaparisia power plant  situation there is still in a very very  dangerous position for the update  gabriel elizondo live in ukraine's  capital kiev  now ukraine's president says winter  could be a turning point for reclaiming  ukrainian territory from russian troops  but vladimir zelensky insists to achieve  those goals more weapons and financial  support are needed  there are 90 days ahead which will  decide more than 30 years of ukraine's  independence 90 days will decide more  than all the years of the existence of  the european union winter will determine  our future and what the risks are  well let's speak now to maximilian hess  who's a fellow in the eurasia program at  the foreign policy research institute  he's joining us from london very good to  have you with us on al jazeera uh what  are your thoughts first about these  remarkable advances by the ukrainians  how do you think they've been able to  achieve these breakthroughs so quickly  well thank you for having me the  ukrainians have credited their own  counter-offensive that they announced at  the end of august in the gherson region  in the south they have said that this  has distracted russian forces and caused  russia to have to regroup and position a  number of its most capable and most  technically advanced units down there  which then enabled them to launch this  counter-offensive uh southeast of  kharkiv and just northwest of the main  fighting in the donetsk region as well  the russian telegram community this  group of russian bloggers who often  travel alongside the russian military  and promote it uh had warned of this  however and have been quite apoplectic  and complaining about it so it's led not  only to a battlefield victory but a  major propaganda victory right i was  going to ask you how damaging is this  for for putin inside russia if russians  get to see this it's it's difficult to  see how he's going to spin this  i think what the ukrainians have really  been fighting for on the battlefield for  quite some time now is ensuring that  whatever victory putin may achieve in  eastern ukraine in terms of taking more  territory that it's a pyrrhic victory  that it's one that ultimately has uh  major costs for him and undermines his  governance in the long term this  backlash that we've seen in this  denouncement from these russian  right-wing groups is exactly that which  they've been hoping to lay the  groundwork for now the russian far right  these russian nationalist groups that  have been the ones who've really been  carping about the military withdrawals  they are not politically unified and  where they are it is very heavily  controlled by the kremlin so it remains  to be seen whether that backlash method  method sizes into something but it  certainly feel and quite unprecedented  in the level that we've seen it how  might moscow respond now you think they  have a limited military pool what plans  do you think are being considered or are  going to be put in place to respond to  to these advances  well sadly in relation to a number of  other ukrainian military successes or  announcements of new initiatives for  example in the question the other week  the russians have then unleashed a  barrage of missile strikes on ukrainian  cities and populated centers so that's a  real concern for for the immediate  future that i think people have to be  worried about from khadki all the way to  the west of ukraine militarily to retake  the initiatives uh in ukraine it looks  like the russians are already sending  more troops uh down to the front there  there are areas where they've had more  successes in the city of baku than its  surroundings uh in northeastern donetsk  oblast so this  uh major advance with ukrainians is a  turning point but the russians do still  have the capability to um really put  pressure on the ukrainians and other  points in the front and will likely  launch their own counter-attack in that  area maximilian we also the atrocities  in bucha are you concerned should we be  concerned that such atrocities could  happen again  as the russians withdraw from territory  that they occupied  you know the the russian  atrocities that we saw there largely  came during the occupation though of  course there were even further attempts  to clean up their tracks as they  withdrew and i think that's a real  concern for the ukrainians also the  looting of industrial property  in the area so yes those are sadly real  concerns as i said the the most  immediate one right now is the russians  have precedent for major uh  rocket strikes on urban areas targeting  civilian populations yeah in terms of of  talks and negotiations  we're not there yet of course but what  would ukraine accept if there were to be  any peace negotiations or do you feel  that ukraine doesn't have to make a deal  now that they've made these advances and  are regaining some of their territory  lost  i don't think there's the political will  on either side right now for renewed  negotiations in ukraine if russia came  and offered them a full withdrawal to  the lines before uh the 24th of february  that  may get them interested but but putin  and his regime have sown no intention of  making that offer uh after this setback  or after previous ones so sadly i think  the ball is still really in the court of  what will it take to get putin to change  his military aims and to recognize that  all that he had hoped to achieve in  february is not only impossible but that  he's getting further and further away  from it as time goes on because any such  move would have major domestic political  ramifications within russia i don't  think we should be expecting it in the  immediate future  that is i certainly hope that  the russian army and the kremlin comes  to that realization thank you so much  for talking to us thank you for insight  maximilian hess from the foreign policy  research institute in london  [Music]
 

